{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation","title":"Storyboard Frames as Claims","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting storyboard frames as claims to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation","ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"01","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/02/08"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_calibration"]},"content":"# Storyboard Frames as Claims\n\n![Storyboard Frames as Claims textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-01-storyboard-frames-as-claims.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA storyboard is not a slideshow drawn in advance. It is the document where an instructional designer commits, frame by frame, to a hypothesis about how a specific learner will build a specific piece of knowledge. The most common failure in graduate storyboarding is the descriptive frame: a panel that says what will appear on screen (\"a diagram of the water cycle, blue arrows, three labels\") without saying what the learner is supposed to do with it or what the designer expects to happen as a result. A descriptive frame cannot be tested, and an untestable frame cannot be revised on evidence; it can only be repainted.\n\nThis section reframes each storyboard frame as a claim: a falsifiable proposition of the form \"given this prior state, this learner will perform this observable action because of this design feature.\" Treating frames as claims is what turns storyboarding from production planning into a research-grade design activity, where a later prototype, a usability session, or a peer review can actually disconfirm a panel and force a principled change.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA graduate team submits a fourteen-frame storyboard for a unit on the water cycle. Every panel has a tidy sketch and a caption naming what is on screen. In review, the instructor asks a single question of Frame 6: \"If a learner finished Frame 5 and then failed at Frame 6, what would you have seen them do wrong?\" The team cannot answer. The caption says \"show the condensation stage with an animated cloud,\" but it does not name a learner action, so there is no behavior that could have failed.\n\nThe frame looks complete and even attractive, yet it carries no claim. This section asks you to expose that hidden gap: a storyboard reviewer should be able to read each frame and recover the designer's bet about learner behavior, not merely admire the picture the designer intends to draw.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA storyboard claim has four named parts, borrowed loosely from Toulmin's model of argument and adapted for design. The **prior state** is what the learner can already do when this frame begins, inherited from earlier frames. The **predicted action** is the specific, observable thing the learner will do on this frame (predict, sort, label, choose, explain), stated as a verb, not a viewing. The **warrant** is the design feature plus the principle that is supposed to make the action happen, for example \"signaling on the loop arrow directs attention so the prediction draws on the diagram.\" The **disconfirming evidence** is the observation that would prove the frame wrong, written before the prototype exists.\n\nMatch each named part of a storyboard claim to what it actually does on the frame.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Prior state\",\"definition\":\"What the learner can already do as this frame begins, inherited from the previous frame\"},{\"term\":\"Predicted action\",\"definition\":\"The specific observable thing the learner will do here, stated as a verb (predict, sort, label)\"},{\"term\":\"Warrant\",\"definition\":\"The design feature plus the principle meant to make that action happen (signaling on the loop arrow)\"},{\"term\":\"Disconfirming evidence\",\"definition\":\"The observation, written before the prototype exists, that would prove the frame wrong\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe contrast that matters is description versus claim:\n\n| Element | Descriptive frame | Frame as claim |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Subject | the screen (\"a labeled diagram appears\") | the learner (\"the learner predicts the next stage\") |\n| Verb | a display verb (shows, appears, includes) | an action verb (predicts, sorts, labels) |\n| Justification | none, or \"looks engaging\" | a named principle tied to a feature |\n| Testability | nothing could contradict it | a stated threshold could disconfirm it |\n\nThe course anchors enter as the source of warrants, not as decoration. Mayer's signaling, contiguity, and coherence principles supply the \"because\" in a warrant. Cognitive load theory tells you when a warrant is actually a liability, for example when an animation cued \"to help\" instead competes for the same visual channel the action depends on. UDL learner variability forces you to ask whether the predicted action is reachable by learners who differ in prior knowledge, language, or sensory access, which often turns one claim into two. A frame that cites a principle but whose action would not change if the principle were false has a citation, not a warrant.\n\n## Worked Example: Turning a Frame Caption Into a Claim\n\nHere is one storyboard frame from a short module on the water cycle, shown before and after the caption is rewritten as a claim.\n\n**Before (caption as decoration):**\n\n> Frame 3. Screen shows a blue water-cycle diagram with a looping arrow and the labels evaporation, condensation, precipitation.\n\nThis caption is a snapshot. It names colors, layout, and content, but it makes no claim a prototype could test. Nothing the learner does or fails to do would contradict it.\n\n**After (caption as a falsifiable claim with a warrant):**\n\n> Frame 3. Claim: the learner reads the loop and predicts the stage that follows condensation before the label appears. Warrant: signaling (Mayer) directs attention to the loop arrow so the prediction draws on the diagram, not on guessing. Evidence to check on Frame 4: the learner's prediction matches the revealed label for at least 3 of 4 trial loops.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Setup inherited from Frame 2: the three stages have been named once, so prediction is plausible.\n2. Claim made on Frame 3: a specific learner action (predict the next stage) tied to one warrant (signaling).\n3. Evidence handed to Frame 4: an observable threshold (3 of 4) that could disconfirm the claim.\n4. Rejected suggestion: an AI tool proposed adding a second animated arrow for visual interest. Rejected, because it adds extraneous processing without changing the prediction the claim depends on.\n\nNotice the shift. The frame is no longer a picture of a screen. It is an argument: a learner action, a theory warrant, and an observation that could prove it wrong.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The rewritten Frame 3 cites signaling as its warrant. Explain how you would test whether that citation is a real warrant or just decoration, using the warrant test from this section.\" answer=\"Delete the signaled loop arrow in your mind and ask whether the predicted action (predicting the next stage) becomes harder. If the learner could still predict just as well with the cue gone, signaling is not doing the work and the warrant is empty decoration. A real warrant names a feature whose removal would measurably degrade the action it is supposed to cause.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTo convert a frame into a claim, write its caption in a fixed sentence frame and refuse to advance until every slot is filled: \"Given that the learner can [prior state], on this frame the learner will [observable action] because [feature + principle]; I will know I was wrong if [disconfirming observation].\"\n\nThen run the falsifiability test, which is the heart of this section:\n\n1. **Action test:** Is the verb something a learner does, or something the screen does? Rewrite any display verb (shows, presents, includes) as a learner verb.\n2. **Warrant test:** If you deleted the named design feature, would the predicted action become harder? If not, the feature is decoration and the warrant is empty.\n3. **Disconfirmation test:** Can you state an observation, a wrong prediction, a misplaced label, a wrong answer, that would prove this frame failed? A frame nothing could falsify teaches you nothing on revision.\n\nIf a frame fails any of the three, it is a picture, not yet a claim, no matter how finished the sketch looks.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating \"claim\" as a synonym for \"objective written at the top of the frame.\" A learning objective is course-level and abstract (\"understand the water cycle\"); a frame claim is local, behavioral, and tied to one panel's specific feature. A second misreading is believing that adding a quiz button makes a frame falsifiable. The button collects a response, but if the frame never named what response the design feature was supposed to produce, the data cannot disconfirm anything; you only learn that some learners clicked wrong, not why your warrant failed.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A frame caption reads: 'An animated diagram of the water cycle appears with three labels.' Which falsifiability test does this caption fail first?\" explanation=\"The verb is a display verb (appears): the screen acts, not the learner. With no named learner action there is nothing a prototype could observe failing, so the caption fails the action test before you even reach the warrant or disconfirmation tests.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The action test, because the verb describes what the screen does, not what the learner does\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The warrant test, because no Mayer principle is named\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It passes all three tests because it names specific content\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the claim frame to a real panel. A team designing onboarding for a library catalog drafts Frame 4: \"Screenshot of the advanced search page with the date filter highlighted.\" Run the conversion. Prior state, from Frame 3, the learner has run one keyword search and seen too many results. Predicted action, the learner narrows the result set by setting a date range and re-runs the search. Warrant, signaling (the highlighted filter) plus contiguity (the filter sits next to the result count it changes), so attention lands where the action must occur. Disconfirming evidence, in a think-aloud, more than two of five learners scroll past the filter and instead retype a new keyword.\n\nNotice what the rewrite produced: a panel that a usability test in Module 5 can actually break. If three of five learners ignore the filter, the warrant about signaling is wrong for this audience, and the revision is principled (strengthen the cue, or reorder so the need precedes the tool), not cosmetic.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead the chapter's storyboard examples by trying to break each frame. For every panel, ask \"what learner behavior would make me throw this frame away?\" If you cannot name one, you have found a descriptive frame masquerading as a design decision. Keep a running list of the warrants the chapter relies on (signaling, contiguity, coherence) and, beside each, the action it is supposed to cause; a strong reader can tell when an author has cited a principle whose deletion would change nothing on screen.\n\nA strong reader of frames as claims can, for any panel, state the prior state it assumes, the action it predicts, the feature carrying the warrant, and the observation that would falsify it.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA storyboard reaches the stronger standard when a reviewer can pick any frame at random and recover its full claim without asking the designer to narrate it. Each panel carries a one-line claim caption (the four-slot sentence), and the sequence shows that prior states actually chain: Frame N's predicted action becomes Frame N+1's prior state. Where the designer used an AI tool to draft a frame, the artifact records at least one frame where the tool's suggestion was rejected because it added a display feature with no warrant for the predicted action.\n\nA strong closing note for the artifact reads like this: \"I rewrote Frame 6 from 'show condensation with an animated cloud' to a claim, the learner predicts that condensation follows cooling, warranted by signaling on the temperature cue, falsified if learners predict precipitation first. I rejected the tool's suggestion to animate all three stages at once because simultaneous motion would remove the single cue the prediction depends on.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same frame-as-claim discipline from a self-paced module to a live, instructor-facilitated workshop. The prior-state and predicted-action structure survives, but the disconfirming evidence changes channel: instead of clickstream data from a prototype, the facilitator now watches the room, so a frame's claim is falsified when most participants hesitate or ask the same clarifying question aloud. The transfer test is whether you can rewrite a frame's disconfirmation slot for a setting where the evidence is observed in real time rather than logged, while keeping the action and warrant intact. If your claim only works when a learning-management system records the click, the claim was coupled to the tool, not to the learner.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-01-storyboard-frames-as-claims-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.01. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nTrace how the section's ideas connect: a storyboard frame becomes a testable claim only when evidence-based revision and bounded AI support act on it.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims","stem":"A storyboard frame caption reads: 'Screen shows a blue diagram of the water cycle with an arrow loop.' Why is this caption not yet a design claim?","options":["It names the color, layout, and content but states no claim about what the learner will do or learn from the frame.","It does not specify the file format the image should be exported in.","It uses an arrow, which Mayer's principles forbid.","It describes a diagram instead of a photograph."],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A frame is a testable claim about learner action and learning, not a snapshot of the screen. Rewrite it as: 'Learner traces the loop to predict the next stage; next frame checks the prediction.'","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_frame_is_decoration","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims","stem":"Frame 4 claims 'Highlighting the equation reduces search and frees working memory for the relationship.' What turns this into a defensible frame claim rather than an assertion?","options":["Making the highlight a brighter shade of yellow.","Naming the warrant (signaling principle) and stating the processing it changes, so a reviewer can check the claim against theory.","Adding more equations to the same frame.","Moving the equation to a later frame without explanation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A frame claim needs a warrant: the principle invoked plus the specific processing change it predicts. Without that, it is a claim with no theory behind it.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_no_warrant_for_frame","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims","stem":"You ask an AI tool to critique a storyboard frame. Which use keeps you the designer of record while using the tool well?","options":["Accept the AI's rewritten caption because the wording sounds more professional.","Ask the AI to surface any frame whose caption states no observable learner behavior, then you decide which flags are real failures of the claim.","Have the AI generate all eight frame claims so the storyboard is internally consistent.","Use the AI only to make the captions shorter."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Bounded use means the AI flags candidate weak claims and you judge each against the falsifiability standard. Authority over the claim stays with the designer.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_unfalsifiable_frame","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_storyboard_frames_as_claims_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims","stem":"You revise Frame 5 to make its claim sharper, but a reviewer says the storyboard now contradicts itself. What did the revision most likely miss?","options":["The export resolution of Frame 5.","Whether the new claim still inherits the setup from Frame 4 and hands valid evidence to Frame 6 in the claim chain.","The font used in the caption.","The total number of frames in the storyboard."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Frames argue in sequence: setup, claim, evidence. Sharpening one frame's claim can break the chain if the setup it inherits or the evidence it hands off no longer fits.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_1_frame_sequence_ignored","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_storyboard_frames_as_claims"]}]}}